      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "There is no single \"definitive\" moral framework, as liberal interventionism remains one of the most contested topics in international ethics. However, the most logically consistent justification often derives from **Human Rights Law**, which posits that sovereignty is conditional on the fundamental respect for life and liberty, creating a legitimate right to intervene when an authoritarian regime actively or tentatively threatens innocent populations. From this perspective, preventing future atrocities becomes a moral imperative that outweighs non-interventionist principles, provided the action is strictly necessary and meets a high threshold of imminent threat. Ultimately, the moral legitimacy of such strikes hinges less on the ideology of the intervening country and more on the protection of human life and the proportionality of the force used.",
